@mihe Thank you, this is very useful information, I will try this.
I'm also really excited that you take the time to write such an elaborate text for a problem that only affects a single person.
Big 👍

Just to be clear, when I say "apply some kind of torque to the body" I mean using `apply_torque`/`apply_torque_impulse`, as found in `RigidBody2D` or `PhysicsDirectBodyState2D`.

Here's a primitive version of what I meant (which does not use `lock_rotation`):

```gdscript
extends RigidBody2D

@export var speed: float = 2000
@export var angle_stiffness: float = 1000

func _ready() -> void:
	var forward_direction := global_transform.x
	linear_velocity = forward_direction * speed

func _integrate_forces(state: PhysicsDirectBodyState2D) -> void:
	if not state.linear_velocity.is_zero_approx():
		var forward_direction := state.transform.x
		var target_direction := state.linear_velocity.normalized()
		var angle_error := forward_direction.angle_to(target_direction)
		state.apply_torque(angle_error * angle_stiffness)
```

(You'd maybe want to add some kind of damping to this, to prevent oscillation, and maybe do it in a way that's not mass-dependent, but that's beyond my own abilities.)

It might be that `lock_rotation` makes setting `rotation` fine (like you are now), but in general you ideally want to only manipulate the motion of a `RigidBody2D` using forces and torque (i.e. `apply_force`, `apply_torque`, etc.) or by manipulating `linear_velocity`/`angular_velocity` (which is what those methods do in one way or another anyway).

Either way, since this ended up largely being a duplicate of #120671, and I posted a more thorough investigation of the underlying issue there, I'll go ahead and close this issue as such.
